  "sections": [
    {
      "sectionId": "section-introduction",
      "heading": "Introduction",
      "sourceAnchor": "introduction",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "This guide is for the updated version of the Eladrin published in Monsters of the Multiverse. For previous versions of the Eladrin, see our "
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "Elf Handbook",
              "href": "https://rpgbot.net/dnd5/characters/races/elf/"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Eladrin is arguably the “original” version of the elf in DnD lore, reflecting elves who never left the Feywild. Mechanically, their history in 5th edition has been long and complex, but the current version of the Eladrin is a lot of fun."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The updated version of the Eladrin published in Monsters of the Multiverse is very similar to the version in Mordenkainen’s Tome of Foes. Fey Ancestry and Trance were both updated to the new versions, and Fey Step was updated to not lock you into Charisma, but their theme and defining traits weren’t changed conceptually."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-4",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Eladrin’s defining trait is Fey Step, which provides teleportation similar to Misty Step several times per day. It also provides a rider effect based on your chosen season, which you can change every long rest. The Autumn and Winter options both call for a saving throws, and since the DC is based on your mental ability scores, those options will naturally appeal more to spellcasters. Summer is the go-to option for melee martial characters. Spring allows you to give your teleportation to someone else, but you likely aren’t playing an eladrin so that "
            },
            {
              "type": "emphasis",
              "text": "other people"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " can teleport."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-5",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Fey Step is notably not a spell, which means that it can’t be counterspelled, works for characters who have trouble casting spells like barbarians, and won’t prevent you from casting a leveled spell as an action."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
